#
# `.` takes no operands past the file name in dash, and it is alone in that.
#
# POSIX gives `.` optional operands that become the dot script's positional
# parameters for the length of the file. bash, zsh and ksh93 all pass them;
# dash ignores them, so the dot script sees the caller's `$@` unchanged —
# which means the same script, sourced the same way, reads a different
# argument list here than in any other shell on the panel.
#
# The dot script is written by this file rather than assumed, so what it
# prints is what this run put in it.

printf 'printf "dot sees [%%s][%%s]\\n" "$#" "$1"\n' > d.sh
set -- keep
. ./d.sh extra
printf 'and the caller still has [%s][%s]\n' "$#" "$1"

# With no operand the answer is the caller's list in every shell, which is
# what makes the line above the difference rather than the pair of them.
. ./d.sh

# More operands than the script reads, and an operand that would need
# quoting: neither is passed either.
. ./d.sh one two three
. ./d.sh 'two words'

# `type` on a special builtin says so, where `type echo` does not (#3273).
#
# The distinction is drawn in four of the seven columns and not in the other
# three, so `echo` is the control on every line: a shell that had merely
# adopted a longer phrase would move it too, and a shell that never says
# `special` fails the first line while passing the control. Ours said the
# short sentence in every dialect until #3273, because there was one wording
# and it took only the name.

type .
type eval
type echo

# `command -V` is the same sentence written by another builtin, and it had
# its own road to it.
command -V .
command -V echo
